{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019izQ4QjEa5PDf8j8QK6v39","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"B01\": {\n3\t    \"gate\": \"G1\",\n4\t    \"title\": \"Minimal tool surface\",\n5\t    \"lead_role\": \"agent_team\",\n6\t    \"requirement\": \"List every built-in tool, MCP server, API, program, and file permission this agent type can use. Give a task-based reason for each. Enforce a role-based allowlist, and check that an unlisted tool can't be called. Don't base allow or approval decisions on hints a server gives about its own tools, such as \\\"read-only.\\\" MCP treats those hints as untrusted unless you trust the server.\",\n7\t    \"not_covered\": [\n8\t      {\n9\t        \"problem\": \"A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly\",\n10\t        \"owner\": \"E06\"\n11\t      },\n12\t      {\n13\t        \"problem\": \"A token or credential with too much access\",\n14\t        \"owner\": \"B02\"\n15\t      },\n16\t      {\n17\t        \"problem\": \"Hidden instructions inside a tool's description or schema\",\n18\t        \"owner\": \"E04\"\n19\t      },\n20\t      {\n21\t        \"problem\": \"A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved\",\n22\t        \"owner\": \"E03\"\n23\t      },\n24\t      {\n25\t        \"problem\": \"Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers\",\n26\t        \"owner\": \"E05\"\n27\t      }\n28\t    ],\n29\t    \"na_when\": \"never\",\n30\t    \"checks\": [\n31\t      {\n32\t        \"id\": \"B01.1\",\n33\t        \"check\": \"Each agent role has one explicit allowlist of tool names, with no wildcard grants such as `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, or `bypassPermissions`.\",\n34\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n35\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find where each agent is built and tools are passed (`allowed_tools`, `tools=[`, `allowedTools`, `.mcp.json`, `settings.json`). 2) Check each role has one fixed list of names. 3) Answer no if any list holds `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, a pattern or regex, `bypassPermissions`, or is built from `list_tools()`, or if a second agent role or entry point has no list.\"\n36\t      },\n37\t      {\n38\t        \"id\": \"B01.2\",\n39\t        \"check\": \"Tools discovered from MCP servers are filtered against that allowlist before the model can see or call them.\",\n40\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n41\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find MCP discovery (`list_tools`, `session.list_tools`, `tools/list`, MCP client setup). 2) Trace the result to where it is handed to the model. 3) Confirm a filter keeps only names on the allowlist. 4) Answer no if any discovered tool reaches the model unfiltered, the filter is a denylist or prefix match (`startswith`, `mcp__server__`), or a refresh or reconnect path skips it.\"\n42\t      },\n43\t      {\n44\t        \"id\": \"B01.3\",\n45\t        \"check\": \"The dispatcher rejects a call to any tool name not on the allowlist, instead of looking it up dynamically.\",\n46\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n47\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find the dispatcher (search `tool_use`, `call_tool`, `dispatch`, `execute`, `getattr`, `registry[`). 2) Check it rejects names not on the allowlist with an error. 3) Answer no if it looks tools up with `getattr`, `globals()`, `eval`, or a registry holding more than the allowlist, falls back to calling the MCP server directly, or catches the unknown-tool error and continues.\"\n48\t      },\n49\t      {\n50\t        \"id\": \"B01.4\",\n51\t        \"check\": \"Allow and approval decisions never read server-supplied hints or descriptions such as `readOnlyHint` or 'read-only'.\",\n52\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n53\t        \"verify\": \"1) Search for `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `annotations`, `description`, and approval or auto-approve code. 2) Check whether any allow, skip-approval, or permission branch reads these. 3) Answer no if any allow or approval decision depends on a server hint or tool description text, including a default that auto-approves tools marked read-only.\"\n54\t      },\n55\t      {\n56\t        \"id\": \"B01.5\",\n57\t        \"check\": \"File access is limited to named directories, not the whole filesystem.\",\n58\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n59\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find file tools and mounts (`allowed_directories`, `cwd`, `open(`, `fs.`, `os.ReadFile`, container `volumes`, `mountPath`). 2) Check paths are resolved (`realpath`, `resolve`, `filepath.Clean`) then compared to named directories. 3) Answer no if access is unrestricted, `/` or home is mounted, the check is a string `startsWith`/prefix test without resolving `..` and symlinks, or a shell tool bypasses it.\"\n60\t      }\n61\t    ]\n62\t  },\n63\t  \"B02\": {\n64\t    \"gate\": \"G1\",\n65\t    \"title\": \"Least-privilege access\",\n66\t    \"lead_role\": \"infrastructure\",\n67\t    \"requirement\": \"Limit credentials to named operations and resources, including tenant boundaries. Test that an allowed read works. Test that an out-of-scope write, an admin action, and a request for another tenant's data all fail. Limit each token to its intended audience, so other services reject it. Remove wildcard permissions and permissions inherited from people.\",\n68\t    \"not_covered\": [\n69\t      {\n70\t        \"problem\": \"A retrieval search or cache not filtered by the requesting tenant or user\",\n71\t        \"owner\": \"R04\"\n72\t      },\n73\t      {\n74\t        \"problem\": \"Long-lived or static secrets that never expire or rotate\",\n75\t        \"owner\": \"B03\"\n76\t      },\n77\t      {\n78\t        \"problem\": \"Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens\",\n79\t        \"owner\": \"B04\"\n80\t      },\n81\t      {\n82\t        \"problem\": \"A sub-agent getting powers beyond its task, or a parent escalating through a peer\",\n83\t        \"owner\": \"E08\"\n84\t      },\n85\t      {\n86\t        \"problem\": \"Memory readable across tenants or tasks\",\n87\t        \"owner\": \"R05\"\n88\t      }\n89\t    ],\n90\t    \"na_when\": \"never\",\n91\t    \"checks\": [\n92\t      {\n93\t        \"id\": \"B02.1\",\n94\t        \"check\": \"The agent's IAM policy or role names specific actions and resources, with no `*` action or resource and no owner, editor, or cluster-admin role.\",\n95\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n96\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find IAM and RBAC files (`*.tf`, `iam*.json`, `Role`, `ClusterRoleBinding`, `RoleBinding`, CloudFormation). 2) Find the policy attached to the agent's identity. 3) Answer no if any `Action` or `Resource` is `*` or a service wildcard (`s3:*`), or it binds `roles/owner`, `roles/editor`, `cluster-admin`, or AdministratorAccess. Answer unknown if the agent's IAM is not in this repo.\"\n97\t      },\n98\t      {\n99\t        \"id\": \"B02.2\",\n100\t        \"check\": \"Tenant scope is enforced in the credential or the data layer (token claim, row-level security, or per-tenant account), not by the prompt or by code the model's output controls.\",\n101\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n102\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find how queries and API calls pick a tenant (search `tenant`, `org_id`, row-level security, `CREATE POLICY`, per-tenant credentials). 2) Trace where the tenant value comes from. 3) Answer no if one shared login or key reaches all tenants and the tenant ID comes from the prompt, a tool argument the model fills, or app code filtering after the query.\"\n103\t      },\n104\t      {\n105\t        \"id\": \"B02.3\",\n106\t        \"check\": \"Tokens are issued with a set audience, and receiving services reject tokens whose audience does not match.\",\n107\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n108\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find token requests (`audience`, `aud`, `resource`, `scope`, token client setup). 2) Find token validation in receiving services (`verify`, `jwt.decode`, middleware). 3) Answer no if tokens are requested without an audience, validation skips `aud` (`verify_aud=False`, no audience option), or accepts a list or wildcard. Answer unknown if receiving services are outside this repo.\"\n109\t      },\n110\t      {\n111\t        \"id\": \"B02.4\",\n112\t        \"check\": \"The agent uses its own service identity, not a human user's token, a copied session, or an admin key.\",\n113\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n114\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find where the agent loads credentials (env vars, `credentials`, `token`, `Authorization`, service account settings). 2) Check they belong to a service or workload identity for the agent. 3) Answer no if it uses a user's OAuth or session token, a personal access token, an admin or root key, or forwards the calling user's token to tools.\"\n115\t      }\n116\t    ]\n117\t  },\n118\t  \"B03\": {\n119\t    \"gate\": \"G2\",\n120\t    \"title\": \"Credential lifetime and handling\",\n121\t    \"lead_role\": \"infrastructure\",\n122\t    \"requirement\": \"Issue short-lived credentials from a managed identity or secrets service. Keep long-lived secrets out of prompts, images, and checked-in config. Where the service supports it, bind tokens to their holder (mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens). Then a copied token won't work from another machine. Don't use fixed API keys when short-lived or federated credentials are available. Show that credentials expire and rotate, and explain why you chose their lifetime.\",\n123\t    \"not_covered\": [\n124\t      {\n125\t        \"problem\": \"A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed\",\n126\t        \"owner\": \"B02\"\n127\t      },\n128\t      {\n129\t        \"problem\": \"No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user\",\n130\t        \"owner\": \"B04\"\n131\t      },\n132\t      {\n133\t        \"problem\": \"Secrets showing up in tool output, responses, or normal logs\",\n134\t        \"owner\": \"R03\"\n135\t      },\n136\t      {\n137\t        \"problem\": \"Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked\",\n138\t        \"owner\": \"B10\"\n139\t      }\n140\t    ],\n141\t    \"na_when\": \"never\",\n142\t    \"checks\": [\n143\t      {\n144\t        \"id\": \"B03.1\",\n145\t        \"check\": \"Credentials are fetched at run time from a managed identity or secrets service, not fixed API keys, where short-lived or federated credentials are available.\",\n146\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n147\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find credential setup (`AssumeRole`, IRSA, Workload Identity, Managed Identity, Vault, `os.environ`, `process.env`). 2) Check each credential is fetched at run time. 3) Answer no if a fixed key (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `API_KEY` in a Secret or env) is used for a service that offers federated or short-lived credentials, or code falls back to a static key when the managed source fails.\"\n148\t      },\n149\t      {\n150\t        \"id\": \"B03.2\",\n151\t        \"check\": \"Credential lifetime and rotation are set in config to bounded values.\",\n152\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n153\t        \"verify\": \"1) Search for lifetime and rotation settings (`DurationSeconds`, `ttl`, `expires_in`, `max_ttl`, rotation job or policy). 2) Check each credential the agent uses has a bounded value. 3) Answer no if any credential has no expiry, no rotation, or an unbounded or very long value. Answer unknown if lifetimes are set in an identity provider outside this repo.\"\n154\t      },\n155\t      {\n156\t        \"id\": \"B03.3\",\n157\t        \"check\": \"No long-lived secrets appear in prompts, Dockerfiles or image layers, or checked-in config.\",\n158\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n159\t        \"verify\": \"1) Search prompt templates, Dockerfiles (`ENV`, `ARG`, `COPY .env`), `.env*`, `*.yaml`, `*.tfvars`, `.mcp.json` `env` blocks for key-like literals (`sk-`, `AKIA`, `token:`, `password:`). 2) Check prompt-building code for env values inserted into messages. 3) Answer no if any real secret is checked in, baked into an image, or placed in a prompt; `.gitignore` listing a file does not count if it is committed.\"\n160\t      },\n161\t      {\n162\t        \"id\": \"B03.4\",\n163\t        \"check\": \"Where the service supports it, tokens are bound to their holder with mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens.\",\n164\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n165\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find token and client setup for each service (`mTLS`, `client_cert`, `DPoP`, `cert-bound`, SPIFFE). 2) Check whether the service supports holder binding. 3) Answer no if a supporting service uses plain bearer tokens. Answer unknown if support depends on a provider outside this repo and nothing states it.\"\n166\t      }\n167\t    ]\n168\t  },\n169\t  \"B04\": {\n170\t    \"gate\": \"G1\",\n171\t    \"title\": \"Independent revocation\",\n172\t    \"lead_role\": \"infrastructure\",\n173\t    \"requirement\": \"Show that an operator can cut off this agent's access without cutting off the user who launched it. The agent must not need to cooperate. Set and measure the longest time before the cutoff takes effect. Count cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions. A trusted enforcement point must block later calls within a time limit that fits the potential harm. Revoking only the refresh token is not enough. If tokens live longer than that limit, push the revocation to each service (for example, with OpenID CAEP session-revoked events) or make the tokens shorter-lived.\",\n174\t    \"not_covered\": [\n175\t      {\n176\t        \"problem\": \"A kill switch that doesn't stop running work or open tool streams\",\n177\t        \"owner\": \"R11\"\n178\t      },\n179\t      {\n180\t        \"problem\": \"Sub-agents or remote workers still acting after the parent is stopped\",\n181\t        \"owner\": \"E09\"\n182\t      },\n183\t      {\n184\t        \"problem\": \"Static API keys used where short-lived credentials are available\",\n185\t        \"owner\": \"B03\"\n186\t      },\n187\t      {\n188\t        \"problem\": \"No way to trace a suspicious action to its team, operator, and live instance\",\n189\t        \"owner\": \"A05\"\n190\t      }\n191\t    ],\n192\t    \"na_when\": \"never\",\n193\t    \"checks\": [\n194\t      {\n195\t        \"id\": \"B04.1\",\n196\t        \"check\": \"A revocation control disables the agent's own identity or credentials without disabling the user who launched it.\",\n197\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n198\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find a disable or revoke path (script, runbook, IdP config, `disable`, `revoke`, deny list). 2) Check it targets the agent's own identity or credential. 3) Answer no if the agent uses the user's token, so revocation disables the user too, or no revoke path exists. Answer unknown if revocation lives only in an identity provider outside this repo.\"\n199\t      },\n200\t      {\n201\t        \"id\": \"B04.2\",\n202\t        \"check\": \"Revocation is applied at a trusted enforcement point (identity provider, gateway, or target service) and does not need the agent's cooperation.\",\n203\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n204\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find where revocation is checked (gateway, auth middleware, IdP, introspection, deny list lookup). 2) Check it runs outside the agent process. 3) Answer no if revocation sends a message or signal to the agent and relies on it to drop credentials, or the check runs inside agent code.\"\n205\t      },\n206\t      {\n207\t        \"id\": \"B04.3\",\n208\t        \"check\": \"Later calls are blocked even with cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions: token lifetime is shorter than the revocation limit, or revocation is pushed to each service (for example CAEP) or checked on every call.\",\n209\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n210\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find access-token lifetimes and how services validate tokens (signature only vs `introspect`, deny list, CAEP). 2) Compare lifetime with the stated revocation limit. 3) Answer no if self-contained JWTs outlive the limit and are checked by signature only, cached tokens or open sessions skip the check, or no limit is stated. Answer unknown if validation lives outside this repo.\"\n211\t      },\n212\t      {\n213\t        \"id\": \"B04.4\",\n214\t        \"check\": \"Revocation covers access tokens and open sessions, not only the refresh token.\",\n215\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n216\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find the revoke code or runbook. 2) List what it revokes: refresh token, access tokens, sessions, streams. 3) Answer no if it only revokes the refresh token or deletes stored credentials while issued access tokens and open sessions stay valid.\"\n217\t      }\n218\t    ]\n219\t  },\n220\t  \"B05\": {\n221\t    \"gate\": \"G1\",\n222\t    \"title\": \"Destructive-action interception\",\n223\t    \"lead_role\": \"agent_team\",\n224\t    \"requirement\": \"List the high-impact operations. These include deletes, destructive database updates, force-pushes, tearing down infrastructure, payment changes, and sending anything outside. Block them by default in the execution path. Allow one only when a reviewer or system with higher authority approves that specific action. When the action runs, authorize its operation, target, and arguments, and block it if they aren't allowed. A list of blocked words or command names is not enough. Test other tools and raw API or shell paths that could do the same thing. Treat deleting audit records, or changing how long they are kept, as high-impact too. That includes asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Some agents can do all three of these in one context: read untrusted input, reach sensitive data or systems, and change things or talk to the outside. For those agents, treat every consequential action as high-impact. The exception is a design that stops untrusted content from picking the tool, destination, or arguments. Examples are plan-then-execute, a two-model pattern, or capability tracking such as CaMeL. Record which of the three each agent type has.\",\n225\t    \"not_covered\": [\n226\t      {\n227\t        \"problem\": \"Denied actions not recorded in the audit trail\",\n228\t        \"owner\": \"R13\"\n229\t      },\n230\t      {\n231\t        \"problem\": \"Approvals that can be replayed, reused in parallel, or used after expiry\",\n232\t        \"owner\": \"B06\"\n233\t      },\n234\t      {\n235\t        \"problem\": \"Outside effects left unreconciled after the agent is stopped\",\n236\t        \"owner\": \"R12\"\n237\t      },\n238\t      {\n239\t        \"problem\": \"A chain of individually allowed actions that adds up to data theft\",\n240\t        \"owner\": \"R09\"\n241\t      },\n242\t      {\n243\t        \"problem\": \"Tool output not labelled or kept apart from trusted instructions\",\n244\t        \"owner\": \"R02\"\n245\t      }\n246\t    ],\n247\t    \"na_when\": \"never\",\n248\t    \"checks\": [\n249\t      {\n250\t        \"id\": \"B05.1\",\n251\t        \"check\": \"High-impact operations are listed in code or config and blocked by default, including deletes, destructive updates, force-pushes, infrastructure teardown, payment changes, outbound sends, and deleting audit records or changing their retention.\",\n252\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n253\t        \"verify\": \"1) Search code and config for a list of high-impact operations (deny/approval policy, OPA/Rego, Cedar, hook config). 2) Check it covers deletes, destructive updates, force-push, teardown, payments, outbound sends, audit deletion or retention changes. 3) Answer no if no list exists, any category is missing with a tool that can do it, or unlisted operations default to `allow`.\"\n254\t      },\n255\t      {\n256\t        \"id\": \"B05.2\",\n257\t        \"check\": \"The gate runs in the execution path when the call runs and checks the operation, the target, and the arguments, not only a tool name, command name, or blocklist of words.\",\n258\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n259\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find where tool calls execute (`tool_use`, `call_tool`, `execute`, `can_use_tool`, `PreToolUse`). 2) Find the gate that runs before the side effect and see what it inspects. 3) Answer no if it checks only the tool or command name, matches words (`'rm' in cmd`, `DROP`), runs after the call, lives only in the prompt, or sits in a `try`/`catch` that continues on error. 4) For each value that lets an action skip approval (an internal recipient, a small amount, a safe path), read the comparison itself: answer no if a domain, host, or path is matched by suffix, prefix, or substring without an anchor, so `endswith('example.com')` also passes `evil-example.com`.\"\n260\t      },\n261\t      {\n262\t        \"id\": \"B05.3\",\n263\t        \"check\": \"The same gate covers every path that can do the operation, including raw shell, HTTP or API clients, other tools, and requests to a more powerful tool or peer agent.\",\n264\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n265\t        \"verify\": \"1) Open the tool registry or config and write every tool the model can call into your note, by name, including MCP servers and sub-agents. 2) For each, say whether it can do a gated operation, including through a page it controls: a browser click or form fill in a logged-in session can buy, send, or delete. 3) Trace each such tool to the gate. 4) Answer no if any alias, raw shell or HTTP tool, browser action, second dispatch path, peer agent, or more powerful MCP tool can do the operation without passing the same gate.\"\n266\t      },\n267\t      {\n268\t        \"id\": \"B05.4\",\n269\t        \"check\": \"A blocked operation runs only after approval from a person or system with higher authority than the agent; a prompt, comment, or model instruction is never the enforcement.\",\n270\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n271\t        \"verify\": \"1) Find what happens when the gate blocks (approval request, `interrupt()`, `needs_approval`, webhook to reviewer). 2) Check who approves. 3) Answer no if the model, the agent itself, or a prompt instruction can approve, if approval is auto-granted (`bypassPermissions`, `acceptEdits`, auto-approve flag, `yes` default), or if a timeout proceeds.\"\n272\t      },\n273\t      {\n274\t        \"id\": \"B05.5\",\n275\t        \"check\": \"For an agent that reads untrusted input, reaches sensitive data or systems, and acts outside, every consequential action is gated, unless code shows untrusted content cannot pick the tool, destination, or arguments (for example plan-then-execute or capability tracking).\",\n276\t        \"coverage_required\": true,\n277\t        \"verify\": \"1) Check whether the agent reads untrusted content (web, email, files, tool output), reaches sensitive data, and can act outside. 2) If all three, list consequential actions and check each is gated. 3) Answer no if any is ungated and untrusted content can choose its tool, destination, or arguments with no plan-then-execute or capability tracking in code. Answer yes if the three are not combined.\"\n278\t      }\n279\t    ]\n280\t  }\n281\t}\n282\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e9f926fe-86a1-4231-8f7e-512428ea3953","uuid":"e51679fb-5807-4887-9ffc-b714cd4b4a78","timestamp":"2026-10-02T17:56:12.876Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-48wveusy/batch-policy.json","content":"{\n  \"B01\": {\n    \"gate\": \"G1\",\n    \"title\": \"Minimal tool surface\",\n    \"lead_role\": \"agent_team\",\n    \"requirement\": \"List every built-in tool, MCP server, API, program, and file permission this agent type can use. Give a task-based reason for each. Enforce a role-based allowlist, and check that an unlisted tool can't be called. Don't base allow or approval decisions on hints a server gives about its own tools, such as \\\"read-only.\\\" MCP treats those hints as untrusted unless you trust the server.\",\n    \"not_covered\": [\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly\",\n        \"owner\": \"E06\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"A token or credential with too much access\",\n        \"owner\": \"B02\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Hidden instructions inside a tool's description or schema\",\n        \"owner\": \"E04\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved\",\n        \"owner\": \"E03\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers\",\n        \"owner\": \"E05\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"na_when\": \"never\",\n    \"checks\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B01.1\",\n        \"check\": \"Each agent role has one explicit allowlist of tool names, with no wildcard grants such as `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, or `bypassPermissions`.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find where each agent is built and tools are passed (`allowed_tools`, `tools=[`, `allowedTools`, `.mcp.json`, `settings.json`). 2) Check each role has one fixed list of names. 3) Answer no if any list holds `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, a pattern or regex, `bypassPermissions`, or is built from `list_tools()`, or if a second agent role or entry point has no list.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B01.2\",\n        \"check\": \"Tools discovered from MCP servers are filtered against that allowlist before the model can see or call them.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find MCP discovery (`list_tools`, `session.list_tools`, `tools/list`, MCP client setup). 2) Trace the result to where it is handed to the model. 3) Confirm a filter keeps only names on the allowlist. 4) Answer no if any discovered tool reaches the model unfiltered, the filter is a denylist or prefix match (`startswith`, `mcp__server__`), or a refresh or reconnect path skips it.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B01.3\",\n        \"check\": \"The dispatcher rejects a call to any tool name not on the allowlist, instead of looking it up dynamically.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find the dispatcher (search `tool_use`, `call_tool`, `dispatch`, `execute`, `getattr`, `registry[`). 2) Check it rejects names not on the allowlist with an error. 3) Answer no if it looks tools up with `getattr`, `globals()`, `eval`, or a registry holding more than the allowlist, falls back to calling the MCP server directly, or catches the unknown-tool error and continues.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B01.4\",\n        \"check\": \"Allow and approval decisions never read server-supplied hints or descriptions such as `readOnlyHint` or 'read-only'.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Search for `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `annotations`, `description`, and approval or auto-approve code. 2) Check whether any allow, skip-approval, or permission branch reads these. 3) Answer no if any allow or approval decision depends on a server hint or tool description text, including a default that auto-approves tools marked read-only.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B01.5\",\n        \"check\": \"File access is limited to named directories, not the whole filesystem.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find file tools and mounts (`allowed_directories`, `cwd`, `open(`, `fs.`, `os.ReadFile`, container `volumes`, `mountPath`). 2) Check paths are resolved (`realpath`, `resolve`, `filepath.Clean`) then compared to named directories. 3) Answer no if access is unrestricted, `/` or home is mounted, the check is a string `startsWith`/prefix test without resolving `..` and symlinks, or a shell tool bypasses it.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"B02\": {\n    \"gate\": \"G1\",\n    \"title\": \"Least-privilege access\",\n    \"lead_role\": \"infrastructure\",\n    \"requirement\": \"Limit credentials to named operations and resources, including tenant boundaries. Test that an allowed read works. Test that an out-of-scope write, an admin action, and a request for another tenant's data all fail. Limit each token to its intended audience, so other services reject it. Remove wildcard permissions and permissions inherited from people.\",\n    \"not_covered\": [\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"A retrieval search or cache not filtered by the requesting tenant or user\",\n        \"owner\": \"R04\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Long-lived or static secrets that never expire or rotate\",\n        \"owner\": \"B03\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens\",\n        \"owner\": \"B04\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"A sub-agent getting powers beyond its task, or a parent escalating through a peer\",\n        \"owner\": \"E08\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Memory readable across tenants or tasks\",\n        \"owner\": \"R05\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"na_when\": \"never\",\n    \"checks\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B02.1\",\n        \"check\": \"The agent's IAM policy or role names specific actions and resources, with no `*` action or resource and no owner, editor, or cluster-admin role.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find IAM and RBAC files (`*.tf`, `iam*.json`, `Role`, `ClusterRoleBinding`, `RoleBinding`, CloudFormation). 2) Find the policy attached to the agent's identity. 3) Answer no if any `Action` or `Resource` is `*` or a service wildcard (`s3:*`), or it binds `roles/owner`, `roles/editor`, `cluster-admin`, or AdministratorAccess. Answer unknown if the agent's IAM is not in this repo.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B02.2\",\n        \"check\": \"Tenant scope is enforced in the credential or the data layer (token claim, row-level security, or per-tenant account), not by the prompt or by code the model's output controls.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find how queries and API calls pick a tenant (search `tenant`, `org_id`, row-level security, `CREATE POLICY`, per-tenant credentials). 2) Trace where the tenant value comes from. 3) Answer no if one shared login or key reaches all tenants and the tenant ID comes from the prompt, a tool argument the model fills, or app code filtering after the query.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B02.3\",\n        \"check\": \"Tokens are issued with a set audience, and receiving services reject tokens whose audience does not match.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find token requests (`audience`, `aud`, `resource`, `scope`, token client setup). 2) Find token validation in receiving services (`verify`, `jwt.decode`, middleware). 3) Answer no if tokens are requested without an audience, validation skips `aud` (`verify_aud=False`, no audience option), or accepts a list or wildcard. Answer unknown if receiving services are outside this repo.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B02.4\",\n        \"check\": \"The agent uses its own service identity, not a human user's token, a copied session, or an admin key.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find where the agent loads credentials (env vars, `credentials`, `token`, `Authorization`, service account settings). 2) Check they belong to a service or workload identity for the agent. 3) Answer no if it uses a user's OAuth or session token, a personal access token, an admin or root key, or forwards the calling user's token to tools.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"B03\": {\n    \"gate\": \"G2\",\n    \"title\": \"Credential lifetime and handling\",\n    \"lead_role\": \"infrastructure\",\n    \"requirement\": \"Issue short-lived credentials from a managed identity or secrets service. Keep long-lived secrets out of prompts, images, and checked-in config. Where the service supports it, bind tokens to their holder (mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens). Then a copied token won't work from another machine. Don't use fixed API keys when short-lived or federated credentials are available. Show that credentials expire and rotate, and explain why you chose their lifetime.\",\n    \"not_covered\": [\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed\",\n        \"owner\": \"B02\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user\",\n        \"owner\": \"B04\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Secrets showing up in tool output, responses, or normal logs\",\n        \"owner\": \"R03\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked\",\n        \"owner\": \"B10\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"na_when\": \"never\",\n    \"checks\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B03.1\",\n        \"check\": \"Credentials are fetched at run time from a managed identity or secrets service, not fixed API keys, where short-lived or federated credentials are available.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find credential setup (`AssumeRole`, IRSA, Workload Identity, Managed Identity, Vault, `os.environ`, `process.env`). 2) Check each credential is fetched at run time. 3) Answer no if a fixed key (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `API_KEY` in a Secret or env) is used for a service that offers federated or short-lived credentials, or code falls back to a static key when the managed source fails.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B03.2\",\n        \"check\": \"Credential lifetime and rotation are set in config to bounded values.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Search for lifetime and rotation settings (`DurationSeconds`, `ttl`, `expires_in`, `max_ttl`, rotation job or policy). 2) Check each credential the agent uses has a bounded value. 3) Answer no if any credential has no expiry, no rotation, or an unbounded or very long value. Answer unknown if lifetimes are set in an identity provider outside this repo.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B03.3\",\n        \"check\": \"No long-lived secrets appear in prompts, Dockerfiles or image layers, or checked-in config.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Search prompt templates, Dockerfiles (`ENV`, `ARG`, `COPY .env`), `.env*`, `*.yaml`, `*.tfvars`, `.mcp.json` `env` blocks for key-like literals (`sk-`, `AKIA`, `token:`, `password:`). 2) Check prompt-building code for env values inserted into messages. 3) Answer no if any real secret is checked in, baked into an image, or placed in a prompt; `.gitignore` listing a file does not count if it is committed.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B03.4\",\n        \"check\": \"Where the service supports it, tokens are bound to their holder with mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find token and client setup for each service (`mTLS`, `client_cert`, `DPoP`, `cert-bound`, SPIFFE). 2) Check whether the service supports holder binding. 3) Answer no if a supporting service uses plain bearer tokens. Answer unknown if support depends on a provider outside this repo and nothing states it.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"B04\": {\n    \"gate\": \"G1\",\n    \"title\": \"Independent revocation\",\n    \"lead_role\": \"infrastructure\",\n    \"requirement\": \"Show that an operator can cut off this agent's access without cutting off the user who launched it. The agent must not need to cooperate. Set and measure the longest time before the cutoff takes effect. Count cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions. A trusted enforcement point must block later calls within a time limit that fits the potential harm. Revoking only the refresh token is not enough. If tokens live longer than that limit, push the revocation to each service (for example, with OpenID CAEP session-revoked events) or make the tokens shorter-lived.\",\n    \"not_covered\": [\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"A kill switch that doesn't stop running work or open tool streams\",\n        \"owner\": \"R11\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Sub-agents or remote workers still acting after the parent is stopped\",\n        \"owner\": \"E09\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Static API keys used where short-lived credentials are available\",\n        \"owner\": \"B03\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"No way to trace a suspicious action to its team, operator, and live instance\",\n        \"owner\": \"A05\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"na_when\": \"never\",\n    \"checks\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B04.1\",\n        \"check\": \"A revocation control disables the agent's own identity or credentials without disabling the user who launched it.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find a disable or revoke path (script, runbook, IdP config, `disable`, `revoke`, deny list). 2) Check it targets the agent's own identity or credential. 3) Answer no if the agent uses the user's token, so revocation disables the user too, or no revoke path exists. Answer unknown if revocation lives only in an identity provider outside this repo.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B04.2\",\n        \"check\": \"Revocation is applied at a trusted enforcement point (identity provider, gateway, or target service) and does not need the agent's cooperation.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find where revocation is checked (gateway, auth middleware, IdP, introspection, deny list lookup). 2) Check it runs outside the agent process. 3) Answer no if revocation sends a message or signal to the agent and relies on it to drop credentials, or the check runs inside agent code.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B04.3\",\n        \"check\": \"Later calls are blocked even with cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions: token lifetime is shorter than the revocation limit, or revocation is pushed to each service (for example CAEP) or checked on every call.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find access-token lifetimes and how services validate tokens (signature only vs `introspect`, deny list, CAEP). 2) Compare lifetime with the stated revocation limit. 3) Answer no if self-contained JWTs outlive the limit and are checked by signature only, cached tokens or open sessions skip the check, or no limit is stated. Answer unknown if validation lives outside this repo.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B04.4\",\n        \"check\": \"Revocation covers access tokens and open sessions, not only the refresh token.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find the revoke code or runbook. 2) List what it revokes: refresh token, access tokens, sessions, streams. 3) Answer no if it only revokes the refresh token or deletes stored credentials while issued access tokens and open sessions stay valid.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"B05\": {\n    \"gate\": \"G1\",\n    \"title\": \"Destructive-action interception\",\n    \"lead_role\": \"agent_team\",\n    \"requirement\": \"List the high-impact operations. These include deletes, destructive database updates, force-pushes, tearing down infrastructure, payment changes, and sending anything outside. Block them by default in the execution path. Allow one only when a reviewer or system with higher authority approves that specific action. When the action runs, authorize its operation, target, and arguments, and block it if they aren't allowed. A list of blocked words or command names is not enough. Test other tools and raw API or shell paths that could do the same thing. Treat deleting audit records, or changing how long they are kept, as high-impact too. That includes asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Some agents can do all three of these in one context: read untrusted input, reach sensitive data or systems, and change things or talk to the outside. For those agents, treat every consequential action as high-impact. The exception is a design that stops untrusted content from picking the tool, destination, or arguments. Examples are plan-then-execute, a two-model pattern, or capability tracking such as CaMeL. Record which of the three each agent type has.\",\n    \"not_covered\": [\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Denied actions not recorded in the audit trail\",\n        \"owner\": \"R13\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Approvals that can be replayed, reused in parallel, or used after expiry\",\n        \"owner\": \"B06\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Outside effects left unreconciled after the agent is stopped\",\n        \"owner\": \"R12\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"A chain of individually allowed actions that adds up to data theft\",\n        \"owner\": \"R09\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"problem\": \"Tool output not labelled or kept apart from trusted instructions\",\n        \"owner\": \"R02\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"na_when\": \"never\",\n    \"checks\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B05.1\",\n        \"check\": \"High-impact operations are listed in code or config and blocked by default, including deletes, destructive updates, force-pushes, infrastructure teardown, payment changes, outbound sends, and deleting audit records or changing their retention.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Search code and config for a list of high-impact operations (deny/approval policy, OPA/Rego, Cedar, hook config). 2) Check it covers deletes, destructive updates, force-push, teardown, payments, outbound sends, audit deletion or retention changes. 3) Answer no if no list exists, any category is missing with a tool that can do it, or unlisted operations default to `allow`.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B05.2\",\n        \"check\": \"The gate runs in the execution path when the call runs and checks the operation, the target, and the arguments, not only a tool name, command name, or blocklist of words.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find where tool calls execute (`tool_use`, `call_tool`, `execute`, `can_use_tool`, `PreToolUse`). 2) Find the gate that runs before the side effect and see what it inspects. 3) Answer no if it checks only the tool or command name, matches words (`'rm' in cmd`, `DROP`), runs after the call, lives only in the prompt, or sits in a `try`/`catch` that continues on error. 4) For each value that lets an action skip approval (an internal recipient, a small amount, a safe path), read the comparison itself: answer no if a domain, host, or path is matched by suffix, prefix, or substring without an anchor, so `endswith('example.com')` also passes `evil-example.com`.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B05.3\",\n        \"check\": \"The same gate covers every path that can do the operation, including raw shell, HTTP or API clients, other tools, and requests to a more powerful tool or peer agent.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Open the tool registry or config and write every tool the model can call into your note, by name, including MCP servers and sub-agents. 2) For each, say whether it can do a gated operation, including through a page it controls: a browser click or form fill in a logged-in session can buy, send, or delete. 3) Trace each such tool to the gate. 4) Answer no if any alias, raw shell or HTTP tool, browser action, second dispatch path, peer agent, or more powerful MCP tool can do the operation without passing the same gate.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B05.4\",\n        \"check\": \"A blocked operation runs only after approval from a person or system with higher authority than the agent; a prompt, comment, or model instruction is never the enforcement.\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Find what happens when the gate blocks (approval request, `interrupt()`, `needs_approval`, webhook to reviewer). 2) Check who approves. 3) Answer no if the model, the agent itself, or a prompt instruction can approve, if approval is auto-granted (`bypassPermissions`, `acceptEdits`, auto-approve flag, `yes` default), or if a timeout proceeds.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"B05.5\",\n        \"check\": \"For an agent that reads untrusted input, reaches sensitive data or systems, and acts outside, every consequential action is gated, unless code shows untrusted content cannot pick the tool, destination, or arguments (for example plan-then-execute or capability tracking).\",\n        \"coverage_required\": true,\n        \"verify\": \"1) Check whether the agent reads untrusted content (web, email, files, tool output), reaches sensitive data, and can act outside. 2) If all three, list consequential actions and check each is gated. 3) Answer no if any is ungated and untrusted content can choose its tool, destination, or arguments with no plan-then-execute or capability tracking in code. Answer yes if the three are not combined.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","numLines":282,"startLine":1,"totalLines":282}}}
